// GetPlayInfoParams defines parameters for GetPlayInfo.
type GetPlayInfoParams struct {
	// PositionMs Position on the item's timeline in milliseconds (the book timeline for a multi-file audiobook). Selects which part of a multi-file audiobook to resolve; omitting it resolves the first part. When the minted stream cannot be range-seeked (`seekable: false`), the server also cuts it to begin at this position and reports where in `startOffsetMs`. A seekable stream is not cut: the client seeks it itself.
	PositionMs *int64 `form:"positionMs,omitempty" json:"positionMs,omitempty"`

	// VoiceBoost Request server-side spoken-word loudness normalization (compression plus leveling gain) applied to the stream, for clients without local DSP. Precedence is resolved here, at mint time: when this parameter is present it wins; when absent, the caller's stored setting for the show or book applies. Honored only for podcast episodes and audiobooks, only when the streaming engine supports it, and only when the item's measured loudness is known; the response's `voiceBoost` always reports the actual outcome. Applying it forces a transcode.
	VoiceBoost *bool `form:"voiceBoost,omitempty" json:"voiceBoost,omitempty"`

	// MaxBitrateKbps Cap the stream's bitrate, in kilobits per second. A lossy source already at or below the cap streams unchanged; anything else is re-encoded down to it: a live encode, positioned through `positionMs` rather than by range requests (for a caller that sets `hls`, an HLS presentation the player seeks itself), with no gapless preloading, counted against the transcode session limits like any other transcode. The cap only narrows what the token authorizes: the server clamps it to the caller's own transcode ceiling, and `appliedBitrateKbps` reports the outcome. Honored only when the streaming engine is running; a server without one serves originals directly and ignores the cap. Omitted, streams shape exactly as they always have. Out-of-range values answer 400 `invalid-request`.
	MaxBitrateKbps *int `form:"maxBitrateKbps,omitempty" json:"maxBitrateKbps,omitempty"`

	// Hls The caller plays HLS. A stream the server would otherwise serve as a live encode (a bitrate cap, voice boost, a track carved from a larger file) is then that item's own HLS presentation, wherever the streaming engine renders one: `seekable` is true, `mimeType` is an HLS playlist type, and the player seeks it itself, so `positionMs` cuts nothing (it still selects a multi-file audiobook's part) and `startOffsetMs` is absent. `expiresAt` lies the item's (or part's) length plus a margin away, at least half an hour, as a timeline's does. Every fetch of its playlists and segments re-checks the item as a stream fetch does. The first fetch takes the listener's transcode slot, the one their gapless timelines hold (one a listener, however many renderings), which goes back once every rendering they hold has gone unfetched for a minute and is taken again by the next fetch; a fetch over the limits answers `transcode-limited`. A stream served as the file is, and one the engine cannot render this way (a server whose engine renders no HLS among them), is minted as if this were absent.
	Hls *bool `form:"hls,omitempty" json:"hls,omitempty"`
}

